    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# **Discriminant Analysis and Naive Bayes: Predicting the Direction of Momentum**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BEW8mqkzYI6f"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This notebook builds on our EDA and KNN analysis of the Jensen, Kelly, and Pedersen (JKP) US factor dataset. In the KNN notebook, we tried to predict the *size* of each factor's monthly return. Here we ask a simpler classification question: can we predict whether a factor will have an **up or down month**?\n",
        "\n",
        "We focus on the momentum factor. It has the highest average return of the 13 factors, but it also has some of the worst crashes. For a factor investor like AQR, the most useful thing to know is when to stay out of it. We use this month's returns on all 13 factors to predict whether momentum will be up or down next month.\n",
        "\n",
        "We compare two classifiers that are both based on Bayes' Rule:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)**, which assumes the predictors are normally distributed within each class and share one covariance matrix. We also run **Quadratic Discriminant Analysis (QDA)** as a robustness check.\n",
        "* **Naive Bayes**, which assumes each predictor is independent of the others within each class.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What We Do Here**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Load the data and check its structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Create the features and target variable for our predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Split the data into training and testing periods.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Fit an LDA model and evaluate it.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Fit a QDA model as a robustness check.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. Fit a naive Bayes model and evaluate it.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. Compare the models using accuracy and a simple timing strategy.\n",
        "\n",
        "8. Use ROC curves to evaluate how well each model separates up and down months.\n",
        "\n",
        "9. Check whether the results hold across different time periods.\n",
        "\n",
        "10. Discuss and interpret the results.\n",
        "\n",
        "Our goal is to see whether these classifiers can predict momentum's direction better than simply assuming it will go up, and whether any of them help an investor avoid momentum's worst months."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "l0-_xsrpZen4"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
