    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Next, we divide the data into two time periods. The training data (1951–1999) is used to fit the models, and the testing data (2000–2025) is used to evaluate how well they predict months they have not seen before.\n",
        "\n",
        "We split the data chronologically so that we only use older information to predict newer returns. A random split would let the model learn from 2009 to \"predict\" 2008, which would be look-ahead bias. Unlike KNN, LDA and naive Bayes have no tuning parameter like K, so we do not need a separate validation period."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "TA8AIn63dBd5"
