      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fSvIFO5YdjCR"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 4. Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YVWsMknzdpxg"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Now we fit an LDA model on the training data. LDA uses Bayes' Rule to estimate the probability that next month is \"Up\" given this month's factor returns. It assumes the features are normally distributed within each class, with different means but the same covariance matrix. This gives a linear boundary between \"Up\" and \"Down\" months.\n",
        "\n",
        "We use the training share of Up and Down months as the prior, and we predict \"Up\" whenever the estimated probability is above 0.5."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "G6fIgD_Jdv7P"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# create the model formula using all 13 lagged factor returns\n",
        "f <- as.formula(paste(\"Direction ~\", paste(names(X), collapse = \" + \")))\n",
        "\n",
        "# fit the LDA model using the training data\n",
        "lda.fit <- lda(f, data = df, subset = train)\n",
        "\n",
        "# display the prior probabilities and the linear discriminant coefficients\n",
        "lda.fit$prior\n",
        "round(lda.fit$scaling, 1)"
      ],
