    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "LDA predicts \"Up\" in 267 of the 312 testing months (86%). Because the prior already leans toward \"Up\" and the signal in the features is weak, the probability rarely falls below 0.5. Its testing accuracy of 57.1% is only 1.7 percentage points better than always predicting \"Up\", and it correctly identifies only 25 of the 139 down months. Its training accuracy (64.6%) is about the same as the training share of up months (64%), so the model learned very little even within the training data.\n",
        "\n",
        "The LDA coefficients look large and contradictory (for example, +86 on debt issuance, -76 on profitability, and -59 on investment). Because factors like investment, value, and low leverage are so highly correlated, LDA assigns large offsetting weights to features that carry nearly the same information. We should not read these coefficients as meaningful on their own."
