    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 5. Quadratic Discriminant Analysis"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ABScRRobfPUG"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "As a robustness check, we also fit a QDA model. QDA is similar to LDA but allows each class to have its own covariance matrix, which gives a curved boundary between \"Up\" and \"Down\" months. This is more flexible, but it requires estimating 182 covariance parameters with only 577 training months."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2wpaE_E8fS_i"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# fit the QDA model using the training data\n",
        "qda.fit <- qda(f, data = df, subset = train)\n",
        "\n",
        "# predict the probability of an Up month in the testing data\n",
        "qda.post <- predict(qda.fit, df[test, ])$posterior[, \"Up\"]\n",
        "qda.pred <- factor(ifelse(qda.post > 0.5, \"Up\", \"Down\"), levels = c(\"Down\", \"Up\"))\n",
        "\n",
        "# display the confusion matrix and accuracy\n",
        "table(Predicted = qda.pred, Truth = y)\n",
        "cat(\"Training Accuracy:\", round(100 * mean(predict(qda.fit)$class == df$Direction[train]), 1), \"%\\n\")\n",
        "cat(\"Testing Accuracy:\", round(100 * mean(qda.pred == y), 1), \"%\\n\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
