    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 6. Naive Bayes"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "PQ4S04SXfYrJ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Next, we fit a naive Bayes model. Like LDA, naive Bayes uses Bayes' Rule, but it makes a stronger assumption: within each class, each feature is **independent** of the others. This means it multiplies together 13 separate probabilities, one for each factor. Each feature is modeled as a normal distribution with its own mean and standard deviation for \"Up\" and \"Down\" months.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because our features are continuous returns, the Laplace adjustment does not apply here. It is only used for zero counts in categorical features."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "NfMbDqlgfaqb"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
