    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This table shows what the naive Bayes model learned. Two patterns stand out:\n",
        "\n",
        "* The **means** are very similar for \"Up\" and \"Down\" months, usually differing by only a few tenths of a percent. The direction of this month's factor returns tells us little about momentum next month.\n",
        "* The **standard deviations are larger before Down months for 12 of the 13 factors**. The ratio is above 1 for every factor except accruals and is largest for profit growth, profitability, short-term reversal, quality, and momentum itself. This means momentum tends to go down after **turbulent** months, when factor returns are large in either direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because naive Bayes multiplies these 13 probabilities together, a turbulent month is counted as evidence for \"Down\" about a dozen times. This pushes the probability of \"Up\" close to zero in those months."
      ],
